# Hamstrack backup configuration (HD-187).
# Copy this file to /etc/hamstrack/backup.env on the host, mode 0640 root:root.
#
#   install -m 0640 -o root -g root backup.env.example /etc/hamstrack/backup.env
#
# INSTALL IT, DO NOT COPY IT NEXT TO THIS FILE. `ops/` is a synced path
# (ops/deploy/synced-paths.txt) and a synced directory is replaced WHOLESALE by a deploy, so
# an `ops/backup/backup.env` would be carried to the box and into five retained
# .config-backup/ copies with AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY in them. Since HD-222 ops/deploy/
# apply-config.sh refuses that outright: any placed file whose basename is `.env`, starts
# with `.env.`, or ENDS IN `.env` aborts the whole apply. A stray copy here therefore fails
# every deploy run from that clone until it is moved — including deploys run from a
# self-hoster's own checkout, where `git pull` will not remove it. This TEMPLATE ends in
# `.example` and travels normally; the `backup.env` it describes never does.
#
# Install this file and EDIT it. Do not replace it with a three-line `printf | tee`: the
# comments below are the only place several of these settings are explained, and the next
# person on the box is usually you, at 3 a.m., during a restore.
#
# It is read twice with two different parsers — by systemd (`EnvironmentFile=` in
# hamstrack-backup.service) and by the script itself (`. /etc/hamstrack/backup.env`) —
# so keep every line a plain `KEY=value` with no shell expansion, no quotes that mean
# something, and no `export`.
#
# The two parsers do not agree on what is valid, and systemd's is the lenient one: it will
# happily accept `BACKUP_LABEL=pre-release(1)` or a line with one quote too few, which bash
# cannot parse at all. The script survives such a file rather than dying on it — it logs a
# WARN, falls back to defaults so it can still publish a failure metric, and then REFUSES to
# take a backup with them, because a dump written to whatever the defaults name is not the
# backup anyone asked for and would be reported as a success. The same refusal covers a line
# that parses and then FAILS — `BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=$(some-tool)` with no such tool — because the
# script runs this file in a child shell before sourcing it. **After editing, check it:**
#
#   sudo bash -n /etc/hamstrack/backup.env      # silence means bash can parse it
#
# `bash -n` parses without running, so it finds the first kind and not the second; the second
# shows up on the next run, as a refusal and never as a backup with a default value in it.
#
# **Save it with UNIX line endings.** A CR at the end of a line becomes part of the value:
# `BACKUP_TARGET=s3\r` matches neither branch and the run refuses to start (deliberately —
# it used to upload while never reporting that it had). The repo copy is pinned to LF in
# `.gitattributes`; an editor on Windows can still undo that on the way to the box.
#
# **The environment beats this file.** The script saves any `BACKUP_*`/`AWS_*` it was
# invoked with and re-applies them after sourcing, so
# `sudo BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=manual /usr/local/bin/hamstrack-backup` really does write to
# `manual/` even though `BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=daily` is set below. Without that, a copy taken
# deliberately before a risky migration would land in `daily/` and be deleted in 30 days.
#
# This is DELIBERATELY NOT /opt/hamstrack/.env: that file is passed wholesale into the
# app container by `env_file:`, and none of the settings below have any business in the
# application's environment. The script never connects to PostgreSQL directly — it
# `docker exec`s into the running container and uses that container's own
# $POSTGRES_USER / $POSTGRES_DB — so it needs none of `.env`'s secrets.
#
# Full rationale: docs/design/production-backups-proposal.md (§9.1, §9.2).
# Operator guide:  docs/self-hosting.md#backups

# ── Destination ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Exactly `s3` or `local`; anything else aborts the run before it writes a byte. `s3`
# uploads off-box and is the only mode that survives losing the machine. `local` keeps
# copies in BACKUP_LOCAL_DIR and skips the upload stage entirely — the `upload` metric
# stage is then not emitted at all, and BackupStale watches the `dump` stage instead (it is
# per-stage, so a stopped timer is still visible in `local` mode). Honest and documented as
# degraded: a copy on the same machine does not survive the machine.
BACKUP_TARGET=s3

# Destination bucket. REQUIRED when BACKUP_TARGET=s3; the run fails fast without it.
BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=

# Key prefix. `daily` is what the 30-day S3 lifecycle rule expires; run the script once
# by hand with BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=manual to take a copy no rule ever deletes (before a
# migration that rewrites flyway_schema_history, for example) — see BACKUP_LABEL.
BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=daily

# Names one hand-taken backup apart from another: with a label the objects are
# `<prefix>/<label>-<ISO8601Z>.dump` instead of `<prefix>/hamstrack-<ISO8601Z>.dump`.
# Only A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ - are accepted. Leave it EMPTY here — it is meant for the command
# line: `sudo BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=manual BACKUP_LABEL=pre-hd188-squash /usr/local/bin/hamstrack-backup`.
# A labelled staging file is exempt from the COUNT-based local sweep (which runs in s3 mode
# only), for the same reason `manual/` is expired by no lifecycle rule — but not from the
# age-based one, which bounds every dump this job writes; see BACKUP_KEEP_LOCAL_DAYS.
#BACKUP_LABEL=

# Bucket region. ALWAYS sent, so it must be right for a non-AWS store too: Backblaze B2
# wants its own (e.g. us-west-004) and MinIO usually accepts us-east-1. A mismatch is an
# opaque SigV4 signature rejection, not a message about regions. Blanking this line does
# not turn it off — an empty value falls back to the default below, which is then sent.
BACKUP_S3_REGION=eu-north-1

# S3-compatible endpoint (MinIO, Backblaze B2, Wasabi…), passed as `--endpoint-url`.
# Empty means real AWS S3. This is what keeps the mechanism from being cloud-only.
# **Must be https://** — the run refuses anything else, because an http:// endpoint ships
# the entire database in cleartext and no bucket policy can stop it: the request never
# reaches AWS. Setting it also relaxes the AWS CLI's default request checksums
# (AWS_REQUEST_CHECKSUM_CALCULATION/AWS_RESPONSE_CHECKSUM_VALIDATION=when_required), which
# CLI >= 2.23 sends and several S3-compatible stores reject with an unexplained 400.
#BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=

# Path-style URLs (`https://host/bucket/key`) instead of virtual-hosted
# (`https://bucket.host/key`). Same knob and the same name as the application's own S3
# backend (`app.storage.s3.path-style-access`); most MinIO deployments need it. The AWS CLI
# takes this from a config file and from no environment variable, so the script writes a
# throwaway one — which means credentials must then come from the environment or
# ~/.aws/credentials, not from a profile in ~/.aws/config. If AWS_CONFIG_FILE or AWS_PROFILE
# is already set the script leaves them alone and warns instead.
#BACKUP_S3_PATH_STYLE_ACCESS=false

# `auto` (default), `on` or `off`. After each upload the stored ETag is compared with the
# local MD5 — an end-to-end integrity check that needs no s3:GetObject, which is what keeps
# the instance role write-only. That equality holds for a single PUT into an SSE-S3 bucket
# on AWS and is NOT guaranteed by every S3-compatible store, where a hard failure would
# store the object and then report the run failed forever. `auto` therefore enforces it on
# AWS and downgrades to a warning when BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT is set; `on` enforces it
# everywhere, `off` skips it.
#
# HOW TO FIND OUT WHETHER YOUR STORE RETURNS THE MD5, so `on` is a decision and not a
# gamble. One PUT of a throwaway object, then compare the two strings:
#
#   printf 'etag probe\n' > /tmp/etag-probe
#   aws --endpoint-url "$BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT" --region "$BACKUP_S3_REGION" \
#       s3api put-object --bucket "$BACKUP_S3_BUCKET" --key "probe-$(date +%s)" \
#       --body /tmp/etag-probe --server-side-encryption AES256 \
#       --query ETag --output text | tr -d '"'
#   md5sum /tmp/etag-probe        # equal → set BACKUP_S3_VERIFY_ETAG=on
#   rm -f /tmp/etag-probe
#
# (Drop --server-side-encryption if your store rejects it; note the answer can differ for a
# multipart upload, which is why the script uses a single PUT. The probe key is written
# once and never overwritten, like every other key.)
#
# WHAT `warn` LEAVES BEHIND, which is the reason to bother with the probe: a log line, and
# nothing else. The run reports `upload 1`, no metric records that the bytes went
# unverified, and this unit's journal is NOT shipped to Loki (Alloy collects through the
# Docker socket and this is a host timer). A self-hoster whose store never returns the MD5
# therefore runs permanently unverified with a green dashboard; the only trace is
# `journalctl -u hamstrack-backup | grep UNVERIFIED` on the box.
#BACKUP_S3_VERIFY_ETAG=auto

# ── Credentials ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# On AWS EC2, leave all of this unset: the instance role supplies credentials through IMDS
# and there is no secret to store. Anywhere else — MinIO, Backblaze B2, Wasabi, a laptop —
# the AWS CLI needs keys, and there are exactly two supported places for them:
#
#   1. here, which is why this file is 0640 root:root (systemd exports them, and the script
#      sources with `set -a` so a hand-run exports them too);
#   2. ~/.aws/credentials, which the unit can read because it sets ProtectHome=read-only.
#
# The key needs `PutObject` on the backup prefix and nothing else. Do not give it delete.
#AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
#AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
#
# Off EC2, set this. Without it every AWS CLI invocation first probes the instance-metadata
# address 169.254.169.254 and waits for it to time out, which turns a 2-second upload into a
# slow one for no reason.
#AWS_EC2_METADATA_DISABLED=true

# ── Locating the database container ────────────────────────────────
# Where the compose file lives; the script `cd`s here before `docker compose`.
BACKUP_COMPOSE_DIR=/opt/hamstrack

# Compose file, used only with `ps -q` to find the container — never with `up`, so
# `--remove-orphans` can never be involved.
BACKUP_COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.prod.yml

# Compose PROJECT name, passed explicitly so an implicitly-loaded override file cannot
# redefine the service under us. Empty means "derive it from BACKUP_COMPOSE_DIR", which is
# what compose itself does — so a stack brought up with `docker compose -p something-else`
# will not be found. It fails loudly ("no running container for compose service…"), so if
# you use `-p`, name the same value here.
#BACKUP_COMPOSE_PROJECT=

# Compose SERVICE name of the database. The container is located by service name and
# never by a hardcoded container name, so a rename breaks nothing. If the service resolves
# to more than one container the run refuses rather than dumping whichever came first.
BACKUP_PG_SERVICE=postgres

# ── Local staging ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Staging directory, and the destination when BACKUP_TARGET=local. Created 0700 and every
# file in it is 0600: these dumps contain every user's email and password hash, and the
# globals dump contains SCRAM verifiers that are enough to authenticate as a PostgreSQL
# role. If you repoint this, add the new path to the unit's ReadWritePaths= as well.
BACKUP_LOCAL_DIR=/var/backups/hamstrack

# How many PREVIOUS staged copies survive the sweep. Convenience for a fast local restore;
# the authoritative copies are the ones in the bucket — so this count applies in s3 mode
# only. Under BACKUP_TARGET=local these files ARE the backup rather than a copy of one,
# nothing counts them, and BACKUP_KEEP_LOCAL_DAYS below is what decides how long they live.
# The sweep runs at the START of each run, not at the end — sweeping on the success path
# only means that once the disk is full the run aborts in pre-flight and can never again
# reach the code that would free the space. Because it runs first, the run then adds one
# more: with 2 you will normally see THREE dumps on disk, not two.
BACKUP_KEEP_LOCAL=2

# Age bound in days, applied in BOTH modes and scoped by SUFFIX rather than by name: every
# `*.dump` and `*.globals.sql` in BACKUP_LOCAL_DIR older than this is removed, whoever named
# it. Nothing this job writes is exempt — not a routine copy the count sweep above has
# already spared, not one you labelled (BACKUP_LABEL), not a stray left by a crash — because
# every one of them is a plaintext copy of the entire database sitting on the root volume,
# and one that is copied into every disk snapshot taken afterwards. "Kept because it was
# deliberate" must not become "kept forever", and `pre-hd188-squash-<TS>.dump` (which the
# release checklist prescribes before every risky migration) is exactly the file that used
# to be. A file with any other suffix is never touched, so notes you park in that directory
# are safe.
# Under BACKUP_TARGET=local this is the whole retention; under s3 it is the upper bound
# behind the count sweep. Remote retention is unaffected either way: in s3 mode that is a
# lifecycle rule the host cannot influence, by design.
BACKUP_KEEP_LOCAL_DAYS=30

# ── Sanity limits ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Floor in bytes: a dump smaller than this is treated as a FAILED dump and never
# uploaded (catches a container that printed an error to stdout instead of dumping).
BACKUP_MIN_BYTES=50000

# Minimum number of restorable objects `pg_restore -l` must list in the dump. The PGDMP
# magic is five bytes at the front and says nothing about the rest; this reads the whole
# table of contents, so an archive truncated at 90% fails here instead of during the restore
# that needed it. A floor, not a fit — Hamstrack's schema lists several hundred.
BACKUP_MIN_TOC_ENTRIES=50

# Pre-flight free-space requirement in MB. Checked before anything is written, so the
# backup job can never be the thing that fills the volume the database is on.
BACKUP_MIN_FREE_MB=2048

# ── Metrics & state ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the .prom file is written. Must match node-exporter's
# --collector.textfile.directory in docker-compose.observability.yml. The directory is
# 0755 and the .prom is 0644 on purpose — node-exporter runs as `nobody` in its container
# and cannot read anything tighter, and a metric it cannot read is an alert that never fires.
BACKUP_TEXTFILE_DIR=/var/lib/node_exporter/textfile_collector

# Last-success state files, so that a FAILED run preserves the previous success
# timestamps — "this run failed" and "nothing has succeeded since Tuesday" are
# different facts and the alerting depends on telling them apart.
BACKUP_STATE_DIR=/var/lib/hamstrack-backup
